DAYS = [
 dict(n=15, f='phi', title='Are you the same person you were at twelve?',
      ep=('Crash Course Philosophy', 19, 'trqDnLNRuSc', '8:33', 'Personal Identity'),
      img='locke',
      imgcap='John Locke made memory and consciousness central to personal identity. The strange question is whether a continuing self is a thing, or a pattern that carries responsibility across time.',
      imgalt='Portrait of John Locke by John Greenhill',
      hook='At twelve, you had different cells, friends, fears and ambitions. Yet you say that child was you, and hold yourself responsible for some of what you did. A teleportation machine, a split brain and a Buddhist meditation all make the ordinary answer wobble. Personal identity asks what, if anything, stays constant.',
      q='What makes a person at one time the same person at another?',
      terms=[('Numerical identity','being one and the same individual, not merely similar'),('Psychological continuity','overlapping memories, intentions, beliefs and character linked by the right causal chain'),('Body criterion','the view that the same living organism persists through time'),('Reductionism','a person is not an extra soul, only a brain, body and connected mental and physical events'),('Anatta','the Buddhist teaching of non-self, denying a permanent independent essence')],
      pre=[('True or false: your body is made of exactly the same matter as when you were twelve.','False. Bodies change over time. That does not by itself settle identity: the philosophical issue is which kind of continuity matters, bodily, psychological, or something else.'),
           ('If a perfect copy of you wakes up, is it you?','Not straightforwardly. It may share your psychology, but if there are now two continuers, numerical identity cannot be one-to-two. Parfit uses branching cases to argue that identity may not be what matters.')],
      watch=['Locke\'s distinction between being the same human, the same substance and the same person','The prince and the cobbler thought experiment, and why memory matters to responsibility','How the teleporter and split-brain cases pressure a single, permanent self'],
      fig='''<svg viewBox="0 0 1000 320" role="img" aria-label="Three theories of personal identity and the branching problem"><defs><marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><rect x="20" y="20" width="285" height="110" rx="12" class="s-card"/><text x="42" y="55" class="s-t">BODY</text><text x="42" y="84" class="s-t-sm">same living organism</text><text x="42" y="108" class="s-t-sm">biology continues</text><rect x="358" y="20" width="285" height="110" rx="12" class="s-box-j"/><text x="380" y="55" class="s-t">MIND</text><text x="380" y="84" class="s-t-sm">memory, intention, character</text><text x="380" y="108" class="s-t-sm">psychological chain</text><rect x="696" y="20" width="285" height="110" rx="12" class="s-box-a"/><text x="718" y="55" class="s-t">NO FIXED ESSENCE</text><text x="718" y="84" class="s-t-sm">events and relations continue</text><text x="718" y="108" class="s-t-sm">anatta, Parfit</text><path d="M305 75H350" class="s-line" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><path d="M643 75H688" class="s-line-m" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><rect x="180" y="190" width="640" height="95" rx="14" class="s-card"/><text x="205" y="224" class="s-t-b">BRANCHING TEST</text><text x="205" y="252" class="s-t-sm">one earlier person  →  two equally continuous copies</text><text x="205" y="275" class="s-t-sm">identity cannot be one thing and two things at once</text><path d="M500 130V180" class="s-dash" marker-end="url(#ah)"/></svg>''',
      figcap='Three answers to the identity question. The branching case is the pressure point: two future people can each inherit your psychology, but they cannot both be numerically identical with one past person.',
      insider=['<b>Locke moved identity from substance to consciousness.</b> In <i>Essay Concerning Human Understanding</i> (1694), the same person is tied to consciousness and accountability, not merely the same soul or body. The memory formulation has a circularity problem, because remembering an action seems to presuppose that the rememberer was the one who acted.',
               '<b>Parfit makes identity less important.</b> Derek Parfit\'s <i>Reasons and Persons</i> (1984) treats a person as a body, brain and linked psychological events. In a fission case, identity fails because one person becomes two, but what we care about, psychological continuity and connectedness, can remain. The 2024 debate still asks whether this reductionism captures morality or leaves out a person.',
               '<b>Buddhist anatta is not the slogan “nothing exists”.</b> Early Buddhist analysis treats a person as changing aggregates, not an eternal owner behind experience. The practical point is loosening attachment to a fixed self, while still preserving ordinary causal and ethical continuity. Contemporary scholarship warns that reading anatta as simply Parfit in robes can flatten differences in soteriology and vocabulary.',
               '<b>Identity has a legal and business edge.</b> Memory is not a secure password for a person, and a brand is not a timeless essence either. A safari lodge can change its site, team and offer while retaining continuity through records, promises, visual cues and relationships. For client handovers, preserve the chain of decisions, not a fantasy that the old version must remain untouched.'],
      names=[('John Locke','1632-1704. In 1694 he made consciousness and accountability central to the modern personal-identity debate.'),('Derek Parfit','1942-2017. <i>Reasons and Persons</i> (1984) argued that psychological continuity matters more than an indivisible self.')],
      room='"Which continuity are we protecting here, bodily, psychological, or contractual?"',
      explain=('In 60 words: why does Parfit say identity may not be what matters?','Parfit imagines fission, where one person\'s psychology continues in two people. Numerical identity cannot branch, but memory, intention and character can continue in both. That suggests the thing we value is psychological continuity and connectedness, not a further soul-like fact of identity. The conclusion is unsettling but useful: responsibility and care can track relations.'),
      recall=[('What is Locke\'s key shift?','He distinguishes the same person from the same body or substance and links personal identity to consciousness, especially memory and accountability.'),('What is the branching problem?','If one earlier person has two equally continuous successors, both cannot be numerically identical to that one person.'),('What does anatta deny?','A permanent, independent self or essence behind changing experience; it does not deny ordinary causal and ethical continuity.')],
      use=('A safari lodge wants a total rebrand. The owner says, "If the logo changes, it is no longer us." How do you frame the decision?','Ask what continuity matters to guests: the name, promises, service standards, local relationships, visual memory, or legal identity. Keep the psychological and contractual links that carry trust, then change what no longer serves the lodge. Identity is not a sacred logo; it is a pattern of continuity that can be redesigned deliberately.'),
      further=[('Crash Course Philosophy #19, Personal Identity','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=trqDnLNRuSc','Video · the verified spine'),('Derek Parfit, Reasons and Persons','https://global.oup.com/academic/product/reasons-and-persons-9780198249085','Book · reductionism and fission'),('Stanford Encyclopedia, Personal Identity','https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/','Reference · current map of the debate')]),

 dict(n=16, f='psy', title='Can a questionnaire measure a personality?',
      ep=('Crash Course Psychology', 22, 'sUrV6oZ3zsk', '11:08', 'Measuring Personality'),
      img='binet',
      imgcap='Alfred Binet helped make psychological measurement concrete. A score is useful only when its items measure the intended construct reliably, validly and fairly across people and contexts.',
      imgalt='Portrait photograph of Alfred Binet',
      hook='A client asks whether you are an “ENTP”, as if four letters explain a person better than years of work. Another questionnaire says you are highly open and conscientious, but a bad week changes his answers. Personality measurement is not a crystal ball. It is model-building under measurement error.',
      q='What does a personality score actually measure, and how much should we trust it?',
      terms=[('Trait','a relatively stable pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving'),('Big Five','openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism'),('HEXACO','a six-factor model adding honesty-humility and reorganising some emotionality and agreeableness content'),('Reliability','consistency of a measure, such as internal consistency or test-retest stability'),('Validity','whether a measure supports the interpretation and use made of its scores')],
      pre=[('True or false: a personality test discovers five boxes inside your brain.','False. Traits are statistical summaries of patterns in responses and behaviour, not literal brain compartments. A score is an estimate with context, item wording and measurement error.'),
           ('If a test is reliable, is it automatically valid?','No. A broken clock can be consistent. Reliability is necessary for many uses, but validity asks whether the score measures the intended trait and predicts something relevant.')],
      watch=['The difference between trait theories, projective tests and self-report inventories','How the Big Five emerged from language and factor analysis','Why a test needs reliability and validity, and why personality is more than a label'],
      fig='''<svg viewBox="0 0 1000 330" role="img" aria-label="From observed answers to cautious personality decisions"><defs><marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><rect x="20" y="95" width="190" height="125" rx="12" class="s-card"/><text x="43" y="130" class="s-t">ITEMS</text><text x="43" y="160" class="s-t-sm">“I plan ahead”</text><text x="43" y="184" class="s-t-sm">“I enjoy novelty”</text><rect x="270" y="95" width="190" height="125" rx="12" class="s-box-a"/><text x="293" y="130" class="s-t">SCORE</text><text x="293" y="160" class="s-t-sm">pattern estimate</text><text x="293" y="184" class="s-t-sm">plus error</text><rect x="520" y="95" width="190" height="125" rx="12" class="s-box-j"/><text x="543" y="130" class="s-t">TRAIT</text><text x="543" y="160" class="s-t-sm">conscientiousness?</text><text x="543" y="184" class="s-t-sm">openness?</text><rect x="770" y="95" width="210" height="125" rx="12" class="s-card"/><text x="793" y="130" class="s-t">DECISION</text><text x="793" y="160" class="s-t-sm">fit, context, outcome</text><text x="793" y="184" class="s-t-sm">not a destiny label</text><path d="M210 157H262" class="s-line" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><path d="M460 157H512" class="s-line" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><path d="M710 157H762" class="s-line-m" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><text x="255" y="60" class="s-t-sm">reliability</text><text x="505" y="60" class="s-t-sm">construct validity</text><text x="755" y="60" class="s-t-sm">criterion validity</text><path d="M370 225V280H650V225" class="s-dash"/><text x="385" y="305" class="s-t-sm">measure more than one time and source</text></svg>''',
      figcap='A questionnaire turns answers into an estimate, then an interpretation. Reliability makes the estimate repeatable; validity asks whether the estimate means what we claim and helps with the decision.',
      insider=['<b>The Big Five is a useful map, not a personality periodic table.</b> Factor models summarise covariation in trait words and items. They are robust in many literate populations, but the Tsimane study found poor replication of the usual five-factor structure in a largely non-literate Indigenous society, a reminder that language, schooling and response style matter.',
               '<b>HEXACO asks what the Big Five can miss.</b> HEXACO adds honesty-humility and distinguishes emotionality from some Big Five neuroticism content. A 2021 meta-analysis of 152 samples and 6,828 unique effects found notable conceptual differences and broader coverage, not a simple winner. For a vendor or commission caller, honesty-humility can be a more relevant hypothesis than “introvert versus extrovert”.',
               '<b>MBTI is popular, but its type story is weak.</b> McCrae and Costa\'s 1989 study of 468 adults found no support for truly dichotomous, qualitatively distinct types, although the four indices related to parts of broader trait dimensions. A 2025 review aggregated 193 studies and reported internal consistency ranges of .845 to .921, but noted that structural-validity and test-retest studies were absent from its 25-year sample. Treat MBTI as a conversation prompt, not a hiring or destiny machine.',
               '<b>People can change, and the measurement must allow it.</b> Roberts, Walton and Viechtbauer\'s 2006 meta-analysis found systematic mean-level personality change across the life course, with increases in maturity-related traits especially in young adulthood. A German longitudinal sample of 20,434 people tested four years apart likewise found strong but imperfect stability and age-related change. A score describes a person at a measurement occasion, not a permanent essence.'],
      names=[('Lewis Goldberg','1932-2012. His lexical and factor-analytic work helped establish the modern Big Five vocabulary.'),('Kibeom Lee and Michael Ashton','Developed HEXACO, a six-factor model whose honesty-humility dimension is especially relevant to exploitation and cooperation.')],
      room='"What is the score reliable for, and what decision is it valid for?"',
      explain=('In 60 words: why is MBTI weaker than a trait measure for serious decisions?','MBTI turns continuous preferences into categorical types, implying sharp boundaries that research does not support. McCrae and Costa found its indices were better understood as dimensions related to broader traits. It can start a reflective conversation, but a serious decision needs validated constructs, multiple observations, relevant criteria and humility about context and change.'),
      recall=[('Name the Big Five.','Openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism.'),('What is the difference between reliability and validity?','Reliability is consistency. Validity is whether the score supports the intended interpretation and use.'),('What does HEXACO add?','Honesty-humility, alongside emotionality, extraversion, agreeableness, conscientiousness and openness to experience.')],
      use=('One of your two commission callers scores “highly persuasive” on a quiz and asks for a higher commission. How do you respond?','Do not price a person from a label. Define the outcome, then inspect comparable evidence: qualified leads, show-up rate, conversion, revenue and client retention across enough calls. A personality score may suggest coaching questions, but it cannot replace behavioural data, a fair rubric and a trial period.'),
      further=[('Crash Course Psychology #22, Measuring Personality','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sUrV6oZ3zsk','Video · the verified spine'),('Ashton and Lee, The HEXACO model','https://hexaco.org/','Reference · model and inventories'),('Thielmann et al. (2021), Big Five and HEXACO meta-analysis','https://doi.org/10.1177/08902070211026793','Paper · 152 samples, 6,828 effects')]),

 dict(n=17, f='phi', title='Did your brain decide before you did?',
      ep=('Crash Course Philosophy', 24, 'vCGtkDzELAI', '10:26', 'Determinism vs Free Will'),
      img='hume',
      imgcap='David Hume argued that freedom is not magic independence from causes. It is the ability to act from your own reasons and desires without coercion, a classic compatibilist move.',
      imgalt='Portrait of David Hume by Allan Ramsay',
      hook='In Libet-style experiments, a small electrical shift appears before a person reports deciding to move. The headline says the brain decided first, so free will is dead. But an averaged signal is not a little homunculus making a decision, and timing a finger twitch is not the same as choosing a safari-lodge strategy.',
      q='Can an action be caused and still be free?',
      terms=[('Determinism','the view that, given the past and laws of nature, only one future is possible'),('Libertarian free will','a form of free will requiring genuine alternatives not fixed by prior causes'),('Compatibilism','the view that causal determination can coexist with freedom understood as reason-responsive action'),('Readiness potential','a brain potential that tends to precede self-initiated movement in EEG averages'),('Veto','the capacity to stop or redirect an emerging action before it is completed')],
      pre=[('True or false: Libet showed that the brain makes every important decision before consciousness.','False. Libet studied simple, self-initiated movements and a reported urge time. The finding is relevant evidence, not a complete theory of deliberation, responsibility or long-term choice.'),
           ('If determinism is true, does punishment have no purpose?','No. A compatibilist can distinguish revenge from protection, deterrence, rehabilitation and fair accountability. A caused action can still reveal reasons, respond to evidence and be shaped by consequences.')],
      watch=['The difference between hard determinism, libertarianism and compatibilism','Libet\'s readiness potential and the timing problem','Why the ability to change your mind, or veto an action, complicates the “brain did it” headline'],
      fig='''<svg viewBox="0 0 1000 330" role="img" aria-label="A cautious timeline from causes to action and possible veto"><defs><marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 0z" fill="currentColor"/></marker><marker id="ahm" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><line x1="70" y1="170" x2="930" y2="170" class="s-line" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><rect x="80" y="90" width="180" height="75" rx="10" class="s-card"/><text x="102" y="122" class="s-t">CAUSES</text><text x="102" y="147" class="s-t-sm">history, body, context</text><rect x="330" y="90" width="220" height="75" rx="10" class="s-box-a"/><text x="352" y="122" class="s-t">EVALUATE</text><text x="352" y="147" class="s-t-sm">reasons, goals, evidence</text><rect x="620" y="90" width="180" height="75" rx="10" class="s-box-j"/><text x="642" y="122" class="s-t">ACT</text><text x="642" y="147" class="s-t-sm">choice in context</text><path d="M540 190C540 270 350 270 350 190" class="s-line-m" marker-end="url(#ahm)"/><text x="377" y="293" class="s-t-sm">revise, inhibit, choose another response</text><text x="70" y="220" class="s-t-b">DETERMINED DOES NOT MEAN COERCED</text><text x="70" y="250" class="s-t-sm">Freedom can mean acting from your own reasons, with the capacity to respond to reasons.</text></svg>''',
      figcap='A decision has causes all the way down. Compatibilism locates freedom in the agent\'s reason-responsive control, including the capacity to revise or veto, not in an uncaused ghost outside nature.',
      insider=['<b>Libet\'s result is narrower than the headline.</b> In the 1980s, participants flexed a wrist whenever they felt like it and reported the urge time while EEG recorded the readiness potential. The task is arbitrary and timing reports are noisy. It does not show that a complex choice, such as taking a commission client, was settled before conscious deliberation.',
               '<b>Schurger changed the interpretation of the buildup.</b> Schurger, Sitt and Dehaene (2012) modelled the readiness potential as a leaky stochastic accumulator. Random subthreshold fluctuations can, when time-locked to movement, look like a smooth buildup before the threshold crossing. Their account explains the signal without treating it as a completed unconscious decision. It challenges, but does not settle, the free-will debate.',
               '<b>Compatibilism is not “you could have done otherwise in exactly the same universe”.</b> Hume and contemporary compatibilists instead emphasise acting from one\'s own reasons without coercion, and being responsive to evidence and consequences. Hard incompatibilists reply that this is not the deep authorship people want. Libertarians reply that genuine alternatives require more than causal responsiveness.',
               '<b>Survey the philosophers, but do not confuse a vote with a proof.</b> The 2020 PhilPapers Survey had 1,758 respondents for this question: 59.16% accepted or leaned toward compatibilism, 18.83% toward libertarianism and 11.21% toward no free will. That is a snapshot of professional opinion, not an experiment. For MTRX, the useful conclusion is practical: design environments where good reasons are visible and bad defaults are interruptible.'],
      names=[('Benjamin Libet','1916-2007. His 1980s EEG experiments made the readiness potential famous in popular free-will debates.'),('Aaron Schurger','Neuroscientist who co-authored the 2012 accumulator model that offers a different interpretation of premovement EEG buildup.')],
      room='"Was the action caused, or was it coerced? Those are not the same question."',
      explain=('In 60 words: what does Schurger\'s model change?','Libet treated the readiness potential as evidence that unconscious motor preparation precedes conscious intention. Schurger, Sitt and Dehaene modelled the average buildup as the result of spontaneous fluctuations accumulating until a threshold is crossed. That can produce the same EEG shape without a settled unconscious decision. It weakens a headline argument, but does not prove metaphysical freedom.'),
      recall=[('What is compatibilism?','The view that an action can be determined and still free when it flows from the person\'s reasons and desires without coercion.'),('What is the readiness potential?','An EEG potential that tends to precede self-initiated movement, especially in averaged data.'),('What did the 2020 PhilPapers Survey find?','Among 1,758 respondents, 59.16% accepted or leaned toward compatibilism, 18.83% libertarianism and 11.21% no free will.')],
      use=('A caller says, "The client rejected my proposal because the algorithm made them do it, so I have no responsibility for the copy." How do you answer?','The algorithm, prior habits and context are causes, not an automatic excuse. Ask whether the caller could inspect the evidence, respond to feedback and change the copy. Build that control into the workflow: show the metric, require a second look before sending, and record why the final choice was made. Responsibility grows with reason-responsive control.'),
      further=[('Crash Course Philosophy #24, Determinism vs Free Will','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vCGtkDzELAI','Video · the verified spine'),('Stanford Encyclopedia, Free Will','https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/','Reference · positions and arguments'),('Schurger, Sitt and Dehaene (2012), accumulator model','https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1210467109','Paper · reinterpretation of the readiness potential')]),

 dict(n=18, f='psy', title='How does a notification train you?',
      ep=('Crash Course Psychology', 11, 'qG2SwE_6uVM', '11:49', 'How to Train a Brain'),
      img='pavlov',
      imgcap='Ivan Pavlov studied salivation, not smartphone addiction. His conditioning experiments gave psychology a durable grammar for how cues acquire power and how consequences shape repetition.',
      imgalt='Portrait photograph of Ivan Pavlov',
      hook='A phone buzzes in a quiet room and you reach for it before you know why. Sometimes there is a useful message, sometimes nothing. The uncertainty keeps the checking loop alive, like a tiny slot machine in a pocket. Conditioning is not mind control, but it can make a cue recruit attention before reflection catches up.',
      q='How do cues and consequences turn a chosen action into a habit?',
      terms=[('Classical conditioning','learning that one stimulus predicts another, so a formerly neutral cue elicits a response'),('Operant conditioning','learning in which consequences change the future probability of a behaviour'),('Conditioned stimulus','a formerly neutral cue that acquires predictive meaning'),('Variable-ratio schedule','reinforcement after an unpredictable number of responses'),('Extinction','the reduction of a learned response when its expected consequence stops arriving')],
      pre=[('True or false: conditioning means the learner is passive and has no thoughts.','False. Classical and operant procedures reveal learning mechanisms, but attention, expectation, memory, goals and context also shape what is learned.'),
           ('If a reward stops, does a learned behaviour disappear immediately?','Usually not. Intermittent reinforcement can make behaviour persistent, and spontaneous recovery can bring an extinguished response back after a pause. Context and new learning matter.')],
      watch=['Pavlov\'s bell, salivation and the distinction between unconditioned and conditioned responses','Skinner\'s operant chamber, reinforcement versus punishment, and shaping','Why variable schedules and extinction explain slot machines, notifications and stubborn habits'],
      fig='''<svg viewBox="0 0 1000 350" role="img" aria-label="From cue to action to consequence, showing classical and operant conditioning"><defs><marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><rect x="25" y="30" width="270" height="105" rx="12" class="s-card"/><text x="48" y="65" class="s-t">CLASSICAL</text><text x="48" y="94" class="s-t-sm">bell + food → salivation</text><text x="48" y="117" class="s-t-sm">cue predicts an event</text><rect x="365" y="30" width="270" height="105" rx="12" class="s-box-a"/><text x="388" y="65" class="s-t">OPERANT</text><text x="388" y="94" class="s-t-sm">action → consequence</text><text x="388" y="117" class="s-t-sm">outcome changes repetition</text><rect x="705" y="30" width="270" height="105" rx="12" class="s-box-j"/><text x="728" y="65" class="s-t">DIGITAL LOOP</text><text x="728" y="94" class="s-t-sm">buzz → check → variable news</text><text x="728" y="117" class="s-t-sm">uncertainty captures attention</text><path d="M295 82H357" class="s-line" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><path d="M635 82H697" class="s-line-m" marker-end="url(#ah)"/><rect x="155" y="205" width="690" height="105" rx="14" class="s-card"/><text x="185" y="240" class="s-t-b">BREAK THE LOOP</text><text x="185" y="268" class="s-t-sm">remove or mute cue  →  add friction  →  choose a replacement reward</text><text x="185" y="293" class="s-t-sm">track the trigger, not just the willpower failure</text><path d="M840 135V190" class="s-dash" marker-end="url(#ah)"/></svg>''',
      figcap='Classical conditioning links a cue to a predicted event. Operant conditioning links an action to its consequence. A notification system can use both: the buzz predicts social information, and checking sometimes pays off.',
      insider=['<b>Pavlov is predictive learning, not a bell-makes-dogs-drool myth.</b> A neutral cue becomes conditioned when it reliably predicts an unconditioned event. Timing, contingency and context matter, and extinction is new learning that the old prediction no longer holds, not a magical eraser of the original association.',
               '<b>Skinner\'s schedules explain persistence.</b> In a variable-ratio schedule, a reward follows an unpredictable number of responses. Ferster and Skinner\'s 1957 work formalised schedules of reinforcement, including why intermittent consequences can produce high and persistent response rates. Slot machines are the obvious example; a notification feed can borrow the same uncertainty without being literally identical to a casino schedule.',
               '<b>The dopamine story needs care.</b> Modern reward-learning work distinguishes prediction error and cue incentive from a simple “pleasure chemical” story. In a gambling-like rat study, Zack and colleagues reported cue-linked effects and modest but consistent sensitisation under a 50% reward-predictive schedule. That is animal evidence about mechanisms, not proof that every app is addictive or that a notification delivers a fixed dopamine hit.',
               '<b>Design the environment, not a morality lecture.</b> Natasha Dow Schull\'s <i>Addiction by Design</i> shows how machine design, speed and continuous play can narrow attention around gambling. For a safari-lodge site, ethical conditioning means making the desired action clear and rewarding, not hiding prices behind an endless tease. For you: mute nonessential notifications, batch checks and replace the cue with a deliberate review window.'],
      names=[('Ivan Pavlov','1849-1936. Physiologist whose work on conditioned reflexes won the 1904 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.'),('B. F. Skinner','1904-1990. Behaviourist who studied operant conditioning and schedules of reinforcement in controlled environments.')],
      room='"What cue is doing the recruiting, and what consequence is teaching the loop?"',
      explain=('In 60 words: why can variable rewards be sticky?','A variable-ratio schedule makes the next reward unpredictable, so each response can still seem worth trying. The action is reinforced intermittently and often resists extinction. Phones are not identical to slot machines, but unpredictable messages can create a cue-check-reward loop. Add friction, predictable checking windows and a replacement reward to redesign the habit.'),
      recall=[('What is the difference between classical and operant conditioning?','Classical conditioning links a cue to a predicted event. Operant conditioning links a behaviour to consequences that change its future probability.'),('What is a variable-ratio schedule?','A schedule that reinforces after an unpredictable number of responses, as in many gambling systems.'),('What is extinction?','A decline in a learned response when the expected consequence no longer follows, although old learning can return in some contexts.')],
      use=('Your safari-lodge client wants more direct bookings. The team proposes fake “only one room left” alerts and random discount pop-ups. What would you recommend?','Use ethical operant design: one clear booking action, transparent availability, a real price and a useful confirmation reward. Test page speed, copy and trust signals. Do not manufacture scarcity or variable discounts that train compulsive checking. Sustainable conversion comes from accurate cues and reliable value, not a conditioning trap that damages trust.'),
      further=[('Crash Course Psychology #11, How to Train a Brain','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qG2SwE_6uVM','Video · the verified spine'),('Natasha Dow Schull, Addiction by Design','https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691160887/addiction-by-design','Book · machine gambling and engineered attention'),('Ferster and Skinner, Schedules of Reinforcement','https://www.bfskinner.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/05/Schedules_of_Reinforcement_PDF.pdf','Reference · primary schedule taxonomy')])
]

